#!/usr/bin/env bash
# inbox-push -- the single tested path for delivering a LIVE tmux notification
# to an agent's pane on a DM send. Centralises the send-keys footgun class so no
# caller hand-rolls send-keys again:
#   - runtime gate     (only poke confirmed claude-runtime panes)
#   - idle gate        (never type into a mid-turn pane)
#   - clean-composer   (never concatenate onto staged/ghost text)
#   - separate keys    (text, then a sleep, then Enter -- no text+Enter race)
#   - capture-VERIFY   (re-read the pane and confirm the paste landed)
#
# PERSIST vs PUSH: this script ONLY does the best-effort live PUSH. The caller
# (inbox reply) has ALREADY persisted the inbox block -- that is the source of
# truth and always happens. This push is best-effort over that guaranteed base:
# it NEVER fails the caller's send, it just reports a status and exits.
#
# Usage: inbox-push <recipient> <message>
#
# Exit codes (caller maps these to a status line):
#   0  -- delivered. stdout is one of:
#         "pushed"            -- positively landed (a new turn started).
#         "queued"            -- pane was BUSY at the gate AND still busy after the
#                                send; the runtime queued our message and auto-
#                                submits it when the turn ends (verified live for
#                                Claude Code). We never clear it.
#                                A Codex pane that was busy at the gate but reads
#                                IDLE-with-staged-text afterwards is a DIFFERENT
#                                case and is not covered by this verdict: it falls
#                                through to the same identify-and-retry logic as an
#                                idle-at-gate send, because a turn that has ENDED
#                                leaving our text unsubmitted is a strand, not a
#                                queue. See the paste-collapse note below.
#         "pushed-unverified" -- sent, transition not positively confirmed (or our
#                                text is still staged on a pane whose busy state we
#                                missed). Left in place, NOT cleared -- the inbox
#                                block is the floor. (Standing rule: never Ctrl-U
#                                a possible CC-queued message.)
#
# CODEX PASTE-COLLAPSE (a 2026-07 fix, twice-reproduced live off a real
# Codex pane): send-keys -l of a message long enough lands in Codex's
# composer as a collapsed placeholder ("[Pasted Content 1024 chars]"), not
# the literal text. The retry-Enter identity check below cannot prefix-match
# against that (the composer genuinely does not contain our characters, only
# Codex's own summary of them), so it always read "not ours" and declined to
# retry -- a confirmed-ours message sat stuck forever, with no error, until a
# human noticed and sent a bare Enter by hand. Recovered a second way: parse
# the char count Codex reports in the placeholder and compare it to the length
# of the message we just sent (see the "codex paste identity" block below).
# The same underlying assumption -- "Codex has no verified queue-and-
# autosubmit the way Claude Code does" -- is why a Codex pane that reads
# idle-with-staged-text AFTER the send takes the identify-and-retry path rather
# than the trust-the-queue shortcut: the turn has demonstrably ended, so there
# is no queue left to trust.
#
# CODEX APPROVAL DIALOGS (fixed 2026-09). Codex prints "Press enter to confirm
# or esc to cancel" under every one of its native approval dialogs (shell
# commands, file writes, everything). A pane wedged on an UNANSWERED dialog is
# running nothing and waiting on a human, so check_idle answers IDLE for it --
# and IDLE is the verdict that sends us straight down the deliver path. The
# deliver block is text + Enter, and Enter is the DIALOG'S CONFIRM KEY: the
# text is swallowed by a modal that is not a text field, and the keypress
# answers a permission prompt unattended, in the yes direction. Two guards,
# both Codex-only (gate 3c and the POST guard): a dialog visible before the
# send skips as "dialog-pending" without typing anything; a dialog that appears
# during the send window ends the run at "pushed-unverified" without reaching
# any retry Enter.
#
# The same collision has a second, milder shape on the label side. Before
# bin/pane-idle narrowed its "esc to cancel" marker to lead-of-clause, the
# dialog footer read as BUSY, and busy-at-gate + busy-after-send emits "queued"
# -- a success verdict for a message a modal swallowed, with nothing running to
# queue behind. That reading is gone from this tree, but the POST guard is
# placed so it fires either way; a guard whose job is to withhold a keystroke
# must not depend on a busy classification it exists to distrust.
#
# KNOWN RESIDUAL, documented rather than papered over: the guards close the
# DIALOG shape of a wrong verdict, not the category. Any other pane text that
# makes check_idle answer busy at both captures without a turn actually running
# still yields "queued" -- a message whose composer-visible tail happens to end
# in the elapsed-counter shape, for instance (see the counter regex in
# bin/pane-idle). The send side cannot distinguish that from a real busy turn:
# it has exactly two captures ~1.2s apart and no history. The designed net for
# that residual is the detection side (bin/stuck-pane-sweep.sh), not a wider
# guess here.
#   2  -- skip:<reason>  not pushed; caller treats as persist-only. Reasons:
#         self, pty-runner, no-window, unknown-runtime (not claude, agy or codex),
#         staged (real text in the composer at the gate), unknown-format,
#         dialog-pending (a Codex approval dialog is up: Enter would confirm it,
#         not deliver -- see the dialog block above), ctx-over (recipient's
#         context is at/over the block threshold and the message is not a
#         handover/compaction carve-out),
#         no-pane-idle-lib (the shared bin/pane-idle library is missing/unreadable
#         or older than v3, so there is no idle gate and no dialog detector --
#         skip rather than type blind). NB: a BUSY pane no longer skips -- we
#         deliver and it queues.
#   1  -- error (bad invocation).
#
# Test seams (no live tmux needed for full-flow coverage):
#   PANE_OVERRIDE=<text>  TEST MODE. Use this string as the FIRST (pre-send) pane
#                         capture instead of `tmux capture-pane`. Setting it also
#                         skips the window-exists probe and suppresses every real
#                         send-keys, so the whole flow (gates -> deliver-logic ->
#                         verify branches) runs purely off injected pane text.
#                         Use '' for an explicitly-empty pane (unset means live tmux).
#   RUNTIME_OVERRIDE=<v>  In TEST MODE, exercise the runtime gate with this value
#                         (e.g. "agy", "codex"). Without this var, TEST MODE skips
#                         the gate (compatible with all pre-existing tests). Has no
#                         effect outside TEST MODE.
#   PANE_OVERRIDE_2=<text> The pane text returned for the POST-send (verify)
#                         captures. Defaults to PANE_OVERRIDE. Lets a test drive
#                         the verify branches (busy / staged / clean) deterministically.
#   PANE_OVERRIDE_3=<text> ...and the capture after retry-Enter #2 (POST2).
#   PANE_OVERRIDE_4=<text> ...and the capture after the codex-only Enter #3
#                         (POST3). Each seam falls back to the previous one, so a
#                         test sets only the states that differ.
#   PUSH_DRY_RUN=1        Run all gates + classify, print the verdict, stop BEFORE
#                         deliver. With PANE_OVERRIDE this is a pure classifier.
#   PUSH_VERIFY_SLEEP=<s> override the post-Enter settle (default 0.8).
#   PUSH_VERIFY_SLEEP_3=<s> override the settle after the codex-only third
#                         Enter (default 1.6 -- see that branch for why it is
#                         deliberately longer than the first two).

set +e

TMUX_SESSION="agents"
# Where the agent homes live. bin/create-agent.sh and bin/boot-agents.sh in this
# same package already read AGENTS_HOME for exactly this directory, so reuse the
# name the package has rather than hardcoding one deployment's layout.
AGENTS_HOME="${AGENTS_HOME:-$HOME/agents}"
VERIFY_SLEEP="${PUSH_VERIFY_SLEEP:-0.8}"
# Settle for the codex-only THIRD Enter. Deliberately longer than
# VERIFY_SLEEP, not a reuse of it -- reasoning at the branch that uses it.
# A literal default rather than arithmetic on VERIFY_SLEEP: these are fractional
# seconds and bash has no float arithmetic.
VERIFY_SLEEP_3="${PUSH_VERIFY_SLEEP_3:-1.6}"

RECIPIENT="${1:-}"
MESSAGE="${2:-}"

if [ -z "$RECIPIENT" ] || [ -z "$MESSAGE" ]; then
  echo "usage: inbox-push <recipient> <message>" >&2
  exit 1
fi

# Collapse any newlines/CRs to spaces: the push is a single-line notification and
# a stray newline inside `send-keys -l` would inject a mid-message Enter (submit
# early / corrupt the composer). Defence in depth -- the caller passes one line.
MESSAGE="${MESSAGE//$'\n'/ }"
MESSAGE="${MESSAGE//$'\r'/ }"

SELF="${AGENT_NAME:-}"

# Test mode: PANE_OVERRIDE set (even to empty) => inject pane text + suppress
# every live tmux side effect (window probe, runtime probe, send-keys).
if [ -n "${PANE_OVERRIDE+x}" ]; then TEST_MODE=1; else TEST_MODE=0; fi
# capture() runs inside $(...) command substitutions (subshells), so an in-memory
# counter would never propagate back. In test mode, persist the capture count in
# a temp file so the gate(1)/POST(2)/POST2(3) captures resolve to the right seam.
if [ "$TEST_MODE" = "1" ]; then
  CAPTURE_COUNT_FILE="$(mktemp)"
  printf '0' > "$CAPTURE_COUNT_FILE"
  trap 'rm -f "$CAPTURE_COUNT_FILE"' EXIT
fi

# tx <tmux-args...>  -- a live tmux side effect; a no-op in test mode.
tx() {
  [ "$TEST_MODE" = "1" ] && return 0
  tmux_base "$@" 2>/dev/null || true
}

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# emit <verdict>  -- print the verdict and exit with the mapped code.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
emit() {
  local verdict="$1"
  echo "$verdict"
  case "$verdict" in
    pushed|pushed-unverified|queued) exit 0 ;;
    skip:*) exit 2 ;;
    *) exit 1 ;;
  esac
}

. "$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)/runtime-launch.sh" || emit "skip:no-runtime-launch-lib"
declare -F agent_opts_into_pty >/dev/null || emit "skip:no-runtime-launch-lib"
if agent_opts_into_pty "$RECIPIENT"; then
  emit "skip:pty-runner"
fi

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# check_idle <pane>  -- return 0 (busy) / 1 (idle).
#
# MOVED to the shared bin/pane-idle library in an earlier revision. It used to be
# copy-pasted here and in inbox-poke-all, and the copies drifted: every busy-signal
# fix landed in THIS file only, so inbox-poke-all has been poking mid-turn
# panes it misread as idle. One copy now, sourced by both,
# plus stuck-pane-sweep.sh. All the reasoning for each marker lives there.
#
# Resolved by BASH_SOURCE, not PATH: this script runs from cron and from the
# Monitor tool, both of which have a minimal PATH. The fallback goes through
# AGENTS_HOME (see the config block above) so a deployment that keeps its agent
# homes somewhere other than ~/agents still finds the library.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
PANE_IDLE_LIB="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" 2>/dev/null && pwd)/pane-idle"
[ -r "$PANE_IDLE_LIB" ] || PANE_IDLE_LIB="$AGENTS_HOME/bin/pane-idle"
# shellcheck source=./pane-idle
. "$PANE_IDLE_LIB" 2>/dev/null
# -ge 2, not merely "loaded": find_composer_line and raw_line_for moved INTO the
# lib at v2, so a v1 lib leaves them undefined here -- and an undefined
# find_composer_line prints nothing and exits non-zero, which check_composer_clean
# reads as rc 2 "unknown format", so every push would skip:unknown-format forever.
# A stale lib must fail at the assertion, loudly, not turn into a silent
# fleet-wide delivery outage. The version is compared only after confirming it is
# all digits: a garbage value must fail the check, never crash `[` into an error
# that reads as a pass.
#
# RAISED to -ge 3 (2026-09): this file now depends on the v3 function
# pane_has_codex_approval_dialog, and THAT one fails in the dangerous direction.
# Undefined in bash means non-zero, which reads as "no dialog on this pane", so
# against a v2 lib the dialog guards would answer all clear every time and we
# would press Enter at pending approval dialogs -- the exact keystroke they exist
# to withhold. The library's other callers do not use it and keep their own pins:
# each caller asserts the version it actually uses.
_lib_ok=0
case "${PANE_IDLE_LIB_VERSION:-}" in
  ''|*[!0-9]*) _lib_ok=0 ;;
  *) [ "$PANE_IDLE_LIB_VERSION" -ge 3 ] && _lib_ok=1 ;;
esac
if [ "$_lib_ok" != "1" ]; then
  # Fail SAFE, not silent: with no busy-detection there is no idle gate, and an
  # undefined check_idle returns non-zero (= "idle") -- which would type into
  # every busy pane. The inbox block is already persisted, so skipping the live
  # push costs a notification, not a message.
  echo "inbox-push: pane-idle lib missing or too old (need >=3, got '${PANE_IDLE_LIB_VERSION:-none}') at $PANE_IDLE_LIB" >&2
  emit "skip:no-pane-idle-lib"
fi

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# find_composer_line + raw_line_for: MOVED to bin/pane-idle at lib v2, for the
# same reason check_idle moved there at v1 -- a third caller (the stuck-pane
# sweep's composer-pending tier) needed them, and a third copy of a
# composer-identification rule means the next fix lands in one of three places.
# All the reasoning for each anchor lives in the lib.
#
# check_composer_clean below deliberately stays HERE: see the lib header for why
# the decision function's polarity cannot be shared even though the primitives
# can.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# check_composer_clean <pane>
#   0 = staged text present (dirty) -- skip / our text is sitting unsubmitted
#   1 = empty composer (clean) -- safe
#   2 = no composer line found (unknown format, e.g. non-claude) -- skip
# Lifted from inbox-poke-all, since diverged (this copy has the ›/NBSP/dim-hint
# and rule-anchoring work; that one has only the rule-anchoring backport).
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
check_composer_clean() {
  local pane="$1"
  local pane_raw="${2:-}"
  local composer_line
  composer_line=$(find_composer_line "$pane")
  if [ -z "$composer_line" ]; then return 2; fi
  local after_prompt
  after_prompt=$(echo "$composer_line" | sed -E 's/^[[:space:]]*(❯|›|>)[[:space:]]*//' 2>/dev/null) || true
  # The bare idle composer rests at the prompt glyph + a NO-BREAK SPACE (U+00A0,
  # bytes C2 A0). [[:space:]] does NOT reliably match the NBSP (locale-dependent;
  # under C it reads as two non-space bytes), so a genuinely-empty composer was
  # misclassified as staged -> a false skip:staged that silently suppressed the
  # push to every idle Claude pane. Strip NBSPs explicitly (byte match, locale-
  # independent) before the has-content test so only REAL typed text counts.
  after_prompt=$(printf '%s' "$after_prompt" | sed 's/\xc2\xa0//g' 2>/dev/null) || true
  # Publish the captured composer text so callers can ask WHOSE it is. This
  # function only ever answers "is there text"; the retry path needs "is it
  # ours", and without this it cannot tell the difference.
  COMPOSER_TEXT="$after_prompt"
  # Known-literal fallback for one specific Codex hint (kept for callers that
  # don't pass pane_raw, e.g. older test seams). Superseded below by the
  # general ANSI-dim check for anyone who does.
  if [ "$after_prompt" = "Implement {feature}" ]; then return 1; fi
  if echo "$after_prompt" | grep -qE '[^[:space:]]' 2>/dev/null; then
    # Codex's idle composer is never bare -- it rotates through several
    # placeholder example prompts ("Implement {feature}", "Run /review on my
    # current changes", etc, confirmed live off a real Codex pane: an
    # exact-string allowlist for one of these missed the others and false-
    # skipped a genuine idle pane as staged). Codex renders every one of
    # these hints wrapped in the ANSI "dim" SGR code (ESC[2m); real
    # user-typed text never is. Check the raw (-e) capture for that marker
    # on the composer line before declaring staged -- this covers ANY
    # rotating hint text, not just one literal, and generalises to any other
    # runtime that uses the same dim-hint convention.
    if [ -n "$pane_raw" ]; then
      local raw_composer_line
      # The raw counterpart of the composer line we just read, matched by
      # content (see raw_line_for) -- NOT "the last glyph line in the raw
      # capture", which is a different line whenever anything glyph-bearing
      # renders below the composer.
      raw_composer_line=$(raw_line_for "$pane_raw" "$composer_line")
      if printf '%s' "$raw_composer_line" | grep -qF "$(printf '\033[2m')" 2>/dev/null; then
        return 1  # dim placeholder hint, not real staged text
      fi
    fi
    return 0
  fi
  return 1  # clean
}

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# capture <agent>  -- pane text, honouring PANE_OVERRIDE for tests.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
capture() {
  if [ "$TEST_MODE" = "1" ]; then
    # The verify path captures up to 4 times: gate(1), POST(2), POST2(3), and
    # POST3(4) for the codex-only third Enter. Each seam falls back to the
    # previous so a test need only set the ones that differ -- which also means
    # a test written before POST3 existed sees the same text at capture 4 as it
    # did at capture 3, i.e. its scripted end-state is unchanged.
    # Counter persists via temp file (we run inside subshells).
    local n
    n=$(cat "$CAPTURE_COUNT_FILE" 2>/dev/null || printf '0')
    n=$((n + 1))
    printf '%s' "$n" > "$CAPTURE_COUNT_FILE"
    case "$n" in
      1) printf '%s' "$PANE_OVERRIDE" ;;
      2) printf '%s' "${PANE_OVERRIDE_2-$PANE_OVERRIDE}" ;;
      3) printf '%s' "${PANE_OVERRIDE_3-${PANE_OVERRIDE_2-$PANE_OVERRIDE}}" ;;
      *) printf '%s' "${PANE_OVERRIDE_4-${PANE_OVERRIDE_3-${PANE_OVERRIDE_2-$PANE_OVERRIDE}}}" ;;
    esac
    return 0
  fi
  tmux_base capture-pane -t "${TMUX_SESSION}:${1}" -p 2>/dev/null || true
}

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# capture_raw <agent>  -- pane text WITH ANSI escape codes (-e), used only to
# disambiguate a Codex dim-hint placeholder from real staged text (see
# check_composer_clean). Honours PANE_RAW_OVERRIDE for tests; unset/empty
# means "no raw capture available" and callers fall back to plain-text logic.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
capture_raw() {
  if [ "$TEST_MODE" = "1" ]; then
    printf '%s' "${PANE_RAW_OVERRIDE:-}"
    return 0
  fi
  tmux_base capture-pane -t "${TMUX_SESSION}:${1}" -p -e 2>/dev/null || true
}

# ==========================================================================
# GATES
# ==========================================================================

# 0. Never push to self (a reply mirrored into our own thread is not an inbound).
if [ -n "$SELF" ] && [ "$RECIPIENT" = "$SELF" ]; then
  emit "skip:self"
fi

# Provider-switch lock: pause delivery while a provider transition is in flight.
PROVIDER_SWITCH_LOCK="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" 2>/dev/null && pwd)/provider-switch-lock"
if [ -x "$PROVIDER_SWITCH_LOCK" ] && \
   "$PROVIDER_SWITCH_LOCK" active "$RECIPIENT"; then
  emit "skip:provider-switch"
fi

# 1. Window must exist (recipient is live in tmux). Skipped in test mode.
if [ "$TEST_MODE" != "1" ]; then
  if ! tmux_base list-windows -t "$TMUX_SESSION" -F '#{window_name}' 2>/dev/null \
       | grep -qxF "$RECIPIENT"; then
    emit "skip:no-window"
  fi
fi

# 2. Runtime gate: only claude, agy, and codex runtime panes take a text+Enter
#    poke. Unknown runtimes have different pane formats and must not be poked.
#    In test mode, RUNTIME_OVERRIDE (if set) drives the gate so the runtime
#    branch is testable; unset = allow through (all pre-existing tests intact).
RT_FILE="$AGENTS_HOME/$RECIPIENT/.runtime"
if [ "$TEST_MODE" != "1" ] || [ -n "${RUNTIME_OVERRIDE+x}" ]; then
  if [ "$TEST_MODE" = "1" ]; then
    _RUNTIME="$RUNTIME_OVERRIDE"
  else
    _RUNTIME="$(cat "$RT_FILE" 2>/dev/null)"
  fi
  if [ "$_RUNTIME" != "claude" ] && [ "$_RUNTIME" != "agy" ] && [ "$_RUNTIME" != "codex" ]; then
    emit "skip:unknown-runtime"
  fi
fi

# 3. Capture the pane.
PANE="$(capture "$RECIPIENT")"
if [ -z "$PANE" ]; then
  emit "skip:no-window"
fi
PANE_RAW="$(capture_raw "$RECIPIENT")"

# 3b. CONTEXT gate: stop pushing at an agent that is nearly full.
#
#     The requirement: stop live pushes to an agent whose context usage is high
#     (default threshold 80%), because an agent that is that busy never gets
#     round to compacting. Handover and compaction messages are exempt and
#     always go through.
#
#     THE CARVE-OUT IS THE WHOLE POINT. Without it this gate blocks the one message
#     that would fix the condition it is gating on, and a full agent can never be
#     told to compact. The exemption is CALLER-SIDE (a flag), never a regex on the
#     body: sniffing for "handover" would both miss a rephrasing and let any message
#     mentioning the word through.
#
#     FAILS OPEN, deliberately, and this is the one place that is right. If the ctx%
#     cannot be read (no pane_ctx_pct, unparseable statusline, non-Claude runtime
#     with no bar) we deliver as before. Failing closed would strand every message
#     fleet-wide the moment a statusline changed -- and this gate is a protective
#     THROTTLE, not a safety gate: failing open restores today's behaviour, while
#     failing closed is an outage. Pinned by its own test.
CTX_BLOCK_AT="${INBOX_PUSH_CTX_BLOCK:-80}"
if [ "${INBOX_PUSH_ALLOW_CTX_OVER:-0}" != "1" ] && [ "$CTX_BLOCK_AT" != "off" ]; then
  if type pane_ctx_pct >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    if _CTX_PCT="$(pane_ctx_pct "$PANE")"; then
      if [ "$_CTX_PCT" -ge "$CTX_BLOCK_AT" ] 2>/dev/null; then
        emit "skip:ctx-over"
      fi
    fi
  fi
fi

# 3c. CODEX APPROVAL-DIALOG gate (2026-09). A Codex pane showing one of its own
#     native approval dialogs is not busy and not idle -- it is WAITING ON A
#     HUMAN, and it is modal. Two things follow, and only the second is about
#     verdict accuracy:
#
#     ENTER IS THE DIALOG'S CONFIRM KEY. The deliver block below is an
#     unconditional send-keys text + Enter. Aimed at a pending dialog, the text
#     is swallowed by a modal that is not a text field and the Enter answers the
#     permission prompt -- in the YES direction, unattended, for a command Codex
#     deliberately stopped to ask a human about. That is a strictly worse
#     outcome than not delivering a notification.
#
#     GATE 4 CANNOT STAND IN FOR THIS. It reads such a pane as IDLE (nothing is
#     running, and the esc-to-cancel marker in bin/pane-idle is anchored so the
#     dialog footer no longer trips it), and IDLE is precisely the verdict that
#     licenses the deliver path. The busy/idle question and the "is a human
#     being asked something" question are independent, and only one of them
#     governs whether it is safe to press a key.
#
#     Skip = persisted-not-pushed, which is the floor the whole script sits on:
#     the inbox block is already written and the caller prints the skip reason
#     generically, so nothing downstream needs teaching about this new one. The
#     dialog itself is not this script's problem to solve -- the stuck-pane
#     sweep's deterministic tier is where a wedged pane gets escalated.
#
#     CODEX-ONLY, deliberately narrow. Claude's own auto-mode confirmation
#     carries the identical Enter hazard, but it is a different literal and a
#     different runtime's dialog, so it is a separate change with its own
#     fixtures rather than something guessed at here. The test suite pins
#     today's behaviour for it so the day it is taken on, the change is visible.
#
#     Runs BEFORE gate 5. Today a dialog pane happens to skip anyway, as
#     "skip:staged" -- find_composer_line lands on the marked selected OPTION
#     line and its text reads as staged. That is an accident of one glyph being
#     reused for two purposes, not a guard: it says nothing about a dialog and
#     would evaporate the moment Codex renders its selection differently. This
#     gate makes the refusal deliberate and the reason honest.
if [ "${_RUNTIME:-}" = "codex" ] && pane_has_codex_approval_dialog "$PANE"; then
  emit "skip:dialog-pending"
fi

# 4. BUSY pane -> deliver-and-QUEUE. A busy CC pane queues
#    typed input and runs it when the current turn ends, so we no longer skip a
#    busy pane -- we deliver and report "queued". The composer-clean gate (5)
#    below still guards against concatenating into a real half-typed/ghost
#    composer (it fires for busy AND idle panes that have staged text).
PANE_WAS_BUSY=0
if check_idle "$PANE"; then
  PANE_WAS_BUSY=1
fi

# 5. CLEAN-COMPOSER gate.
check_composer_clean "$PANE" "$PANE_RAW"
case $? in
  0) emit "skip:staged" ;;
  2) emit "skip:unknown-format" ;;
esac

# All gates passed. In dry-run, stop here with the would-push verdict.
if [ "${PUSH_DRY_RUN:-}" = "1" ]; then
  emit "pushed"   # classifier verdict: gates pass, would deliver
fi

# ==========================================================================
# DELIVER  (separate text / sleep / Enter -- the proven anti-race sequence)
# ==========================================================================
# -l sends the message LITERALLY (no key-name lookup), so a word like "Enter"
# or "Space" inside the subject can never be reinterpreted as a keypress.
tx send-keys -t "${TMUX_SESSION}:${RECIPIENT}" -l "$MESSAGE"
[ "$TEST_MODE" = "1" ] || sleep 0.4
tx send-keys -t "${TMUX_SESSION}:${RECIPIENT}" Enter

# ==========================================================================
# VERIFY  (synchronous -- so the sender never has to poll)
# ==========================================================================
[ "$TEST_MODE" = "1" ] || sleep "$VERIFY_SLEEP"
POST="$(capture "$RECIPIENT")"
POST_RAW="$(capture_raw "$RECIPIENT")"

# A Codex approval dialog on the POST capture (2026-09). Belt-and-braces for the
# case gate 3c cannot see: the dialog was NOT up when we captured the gate pane
# (a turn was running, or the pane was idle) and it came up during the ~1.2s send
# window, so our text and our Enter arrived at a modal.
#
# Everything below this line is wrong for that pane:
#   - the staged/retry machinery would fire Enter #2 at the dialog, and Enter is
#     its CONFIRM key, so a "retry" there is an unattended yes to a permission
#     prompt. Control must not reach that code, which is why this emits rather
#     than setting a flag.
#   - if the pane was busy at the gate and the dialog footer were still being
#     read as busy, the branch below would claim "queued" for a delivery that did
#     not happen: there is no queue behind a modal.
#
# Today only the first of those is live here, because a dialog pane reads IDLE
# (see gate 3c). Do NOT therefore fold this into the check_idle branch below. The
# guard's job is to withhold a keystroke, and it must not depend on a busy
# classification it exists to distrust -- in either direction, since the marker
# that reads this footer has already moved once. The test suite pins the
# ORDERING, not just the verdict, for that reason.
#
# "pushed-unverified" is the honest verdict: keystrokes went to the pane, and
# what became of them is genuinely unknown. The inbox block is the floor.
if [ "${_RUNTIME:-}" = "codex" ] && pane_has_codex_approval_dialog "$POST"; then
  emit "pushed-unverified"
fi

# Positive signal: the pane is busy after delivery. If it was IDLE at the gate,
# the Enter started a NEW turn (delivered + running now = "pushed"). If it was
# already BUSY at the gate, our message is QUEUED behind the current turn and
# runs when that turn ends ("queued").
if check_idle "$POST"; then
  [ "$PANE_WAS_BUSY" = "1" ] && emit "queued"
  emit "pushed"
fi

# Still idle. Did our text fail to submit and get left staged?
check_composer_clean "$POST" "$POST_RAW"
case $? in
  0)
    # Composer holds our text. If the pane was BUSY at the gate, CC has QUEUED
    # it and auto-submits when the turn ends -- VERIFIED LIVE (a
    # mid-turn send queued behind the active turn and delivered after it). Do
    # NOT retry/clear: the old Ctrl-U was destroying CC's queued message (the
    # root cause of the spurious skip:could-not-submit). Leave it queued.
    #
    # Codex exception (from the paste-collapse work described in the header):
    # that auto-submit-on-queue behaviour is
    # verified for Claude only. Trusting the same assumption for Codex would
    # silently strand a message with the sender told "queued" (success)
    # instead of actually stuck -- so a Codex pane busy at the gate falls
    # through to the identify-and-retry logic below instead of trusting the
    # queue. Harmless if Codex DOES auto-submit: by the time we are in this
    # branch its turn has already ended (the check above already ruled out
    # still-busy), so if it had auto-submitted the composer would be clean,
    # not staged, and we would never have reached here.
    if [ "$PANE_WAS_BUSY" = "1" ] && [ "$_RUNTIME" != "codex" ]; then
      emit "queued"
    fi
    # Pane was IDLE at the gate (or PANE_WAS_BUSY + codex, just above): a
    # genuine text-then-Enter race, or a Codex turn that ended without
    # actually submitting our queued text. Retry once, but ONLY if the
    # staged text is actually OURS.
    #
    # check_composer_clean answers "is there text", never "is it our text" --
    # it has no access to $MESSAGE and never compares against it. The comment
    # above claimed identity; the code tested presence. So this branch would
    # fire an Enter at ANY staged text, and if a human had started typing into
    # the pane during the ~1.6s window (send, settle, capture, settle) it would
    # submit their half-written line believing it was completing its own
    # delivery.
    #
    # Normalised PREFIX compare, deliberately not an exact match: a long
    # message wraps across several rendered lines and check_composer_clean only
    # reads the LAST prompt line, so an exact compare would never match a real
    # message and this guard would sit here permanently inert -- safe, and
    # indistinguishable from working. That is the same defect as the spinner
    # literals with the sign flipped, so the test suite proves this can go
    # GREEN on a real wrapped message as well as red on foreign text.
    _norm() { printf '%s' "$1" | tr -s '[:space:]' ' ' | sed 's/^ //; s/ $//'; }
    staged_norm="$(_norm "${COMPOSER_TEXT:-}")"
    message_norm="$(_norm "$MESSAGE")"
    ident_len=24
    if [ "${#staged_norm}" -lt "$ident_len" ]; then ident_len="${#staged_norm}"; fi
    IDENTITY_CONFIRMED=0
    if [ "$ident_len" -ge 8 ] \
       && [ "$(printf '%s' "$message_norm" | cut -c1-"$ident_len")" \
            = "$(printf '%s' "$staged_norm" | cut -c1-"$ident_len")" ]; then
      IDENTITY_CONFIRMED=1
    fi
    # codex paste identity (the 2026-07 paste-collapse fix): the prefix compare
    # above can never
    # match a Codex paste-collapse placeholder, because the composer holds
    # Codex's own summary of our text, not our text. Recover identity from the
    # char count Codex reports ("[Pasted Content N chars]", tolerant of the
    # exact wording per the dim-hint precedent above): if it is within a few
    # characters of the length of the message we just sent, this is almost
    # certainly our own just-collapsed paste, not a coincidence. A stray
    # unrelated paste landing in this exact ~1.2s post-send window, on a pane
    # humans rarely type into directly (channel fidelity: they use the inbox), AND
    # happening to match our sent length within tolerance, is not a real risk
    # this guard needs to defend against.
    if [ "$IDENTITY_CONFIRMED" = "0" ] && [ "$_RUNTIME" = "codex" ]; then
      staged_paste_chars="$(printf '%s' "$staged_norm" \
        | sed -nE 's/.*\[Pasted [A-Za-z]+ ([0-9]+) chars?\].*/\1/p')"
      if [ -n "$staged_paste_chars" ]; then
        sent_len="${#MESSAGE}"
        paste_diff=$((staged_paste_chars - sent_len))
        [ "$paste_diff" -lt 0 ] && paste_diff=$((-paste_diff))
        if [ "$paste_diff" -le 3 ]; then
          IDENTITY_CONFIRMED=1
        fi
      fi
    fi
    # CODEX CAPS THE NUMBER IT REPORTS, so the count compare above is inert for
    # every message that actually collapses (found live, 2026-08).
    #
    # Measured, not inferred: four real messages of 1234, 1795, 2142 and 4072
    # chars all rendered as "[Pasted Content 1024 chars]". 1024 is a CEILING
    # wearing the costume of a measurement. So `paste_diff` is the overshoot,
    # never <= 3, and identity is never confirmed.
    #
    # THE CONSEQUENCE WAS THE WHOLE FEATURE BEING DEAD CODE. `emit` calls exit,
    # so the not-ours branch below terminated the script BEFORE Enter #1 -- not
    # merely before the third Enter the paste-collapse fix added. Codex only collapses
    # LONG pastes, and long is exactly what the cap hides, so the guard failed
    # precisely and only in the case it was written for. Verified live in both
    # directions on the same pane minutes apart: an 829-char message emitted
    # `pushed` with a clean composer, a 1234-char one emitted
    # `pushed-unverified` and sat unsubmitted.
    #
    # This is the failure mode the prefix-compare comment names 30 lines up --
    # "permanently inert, safe, and indistinguishable from working" -- shipped
    # again in the guard written to fix it. Hence the test below asserts a
    # DELIVERY, not a code path.
    #
    # THE VISIBLE REMAINDER IS THE SIGNAL. Codex collapses the FIRST 1024
    # characters into the placeholder and renders everything after them inline.
    # Verified byte-exactly against a live pane: the text following "chars]" is
    # our message from offset 1024, starting MID-WORD and with no separating
    # space.
    #
    # So this is a SUBSTRING test, not a suffix test. Asserting that the
    # composer ends with the end of the message is false twice over:
    # check_composer_clean reads only the LAST PROMPT LINE, and that line holds
    # the START of the remainder, with the rest wrapping below it. The fixture
    # below is copied from a real pane, mid-word break and missing space
    # included, because a fixture built from assumptions about the pane passes
    # its own test while proving nothing.
    #
    # Safe: a foreign paste would have to contain 24+ consecutive characters of
    # the message we just sent. That is a strictly stronger claim than the count
    # compare it backs up, which only ever matched a length.
    #
    # Deliberately NOT keyed on the number 1024. That would enumerate today's
    # ceiling, which is the same defect one layer over (see the spinner-glyph
    # history in bin/pane-idle, three revisions of exactly this). The condition
    # is "a placeholder is present and identity is still unconfirmed", whatever
    # the number says.
    if [ "$IDENTITY_CONFIRMED" = "0" ] && [ "$_RUNTIME" = "codex" ] \
       && [ -n "${staged_paste_chars:-}" ]; then
      # Everything the composer shows AFTER the placeholder. The space is
      # optional because the live pane has none.
      staged_rest="$(printf '%s' "$staged_norm" \
        | sed -E 's/.*\[Pasted [A-Za-z]+ [0-9]+ chars?\] ?//')"
      # 24 chars, the same floor the prefix compare uses: long enough that a
      # coincidental match is not a real risk, short enough to survive a narrow
      # pane showing only a fragment of the remainder.
      if [ "${#staged_rest}" -ge 24 ] && [ "$message_norm" != "${message_norm/"$staged_rest"/}" ]; then
        IDENTITY_CONFIRMED=1
      fi
    fi
    if [ "$IDENTITY_CONFIRMED" = "0" ]; then
      # Not ours (or too little to tell). Do NOT press Enter on someone else's
      # composer. The inbox block is already persisted, so the floor holds.
      emit "pushed-unverified"
    fi
    tx send-keys -t "${TMUX_SESSION}:${RECIPIENT}" Enter
    [ "$TEST_MODE" = "1" ] || sleep "$VERIFY_SLEEP"
    POST2="$(capture "$RECIPIENT")"
    if check_idle "$POST2"; then
      emit "pushed"
    fi
    # THIRD AND FINAL Enter -- codex only. Observed in practice:
    # a message to an agent on the codex runtime sat paste-collapsed and
    # genuinely unsubmitted in its composer: BOTH Enters above failed to
    # register, and one more bare Enter delivered it -- the same keypress
    # this script had already sent twice. So the two-try ceiling was empirically
    # one short for Codex, and the give-up below was reporting
    # "pushed-unverified" for a message that one more try would have delivered.
    #
    # This is NOT a new risk class, and specifically NOT a new place where we
    # might press Enter on someone else's composer:
    #   - it is reachable ONLY with IDENTITY_CONFIRMED=1. The not-ours branch
    #     above has already exited with pushed-unverified before Enter #2, so by
    #     the time control gets here the staged text has been positively
    #     identified as our own (prefix compare, or the paste-char count from
    #     the paste-collapse fix). We do not re-derive that -- we inherit it.
    #   - the pane is still idle (check_idle just said so) and still holding
    #     that same staged text.
    #   - it adds no clearing behaviour. Ctrl-U remains never (see below).
    # Bounded at exactly one extra attempt: if it misses, we fall straight
    # through to the unchanged give-up. Three tries then stop is the ceiling.
    #
    # Claude is deliberately excluded. Its queue-and-autosubmit IS verified,
    # so a Claude pane in this state is more likely holding a
    # queued message than a stuck one, and the reasoning below about not
    # fighting CC's queue still governs. Same gating precedent as the
    # codex-only paste-identity recovery above.
    #
    # The settle is deliberately LONGER than VERIFY_SLEEP (1.6s vs 0.8s). Two
    # attempts have now failed to show a transition within 0.8s, which has two
    # candidate explanations: the Enter is not registering at all, or 0.8s is
    # too short to observe the transition on this pane. A third 0.8s window can
    # only re-test the first; a longer one also tests the second, so it is the
    # only attempt of the three carrying new information. The cost is paid only
    # on this already-degraded path -- a normal send never reaches here.
    if [ "$_RUNTIME" = "codex" ]; then
      tx send-keys -t "${TMUX_SESSION}:${RECIPIENT}" Enter
      [ "$TEST_MODE" = "1" ] || sleep "$VERIFY_SLEEP_3"
      POST3="$(capture "$RECIPIENT")"
      if check_idle "$POST3"; then
        emit "pushed"
      fi
    fi
    # Not busy after the retries. Do NOT Ctrl-U clear, even if our text is still
    # staged: clearing risks destroying a CC-QUEUED message
    # on a pane whose busy state check_idle missed -- its busy-detection is
    # unreliable (e.g. the `…` / `✽` spinner glyphs it does not match). If the
    # text is queued, CC auto-submits it; if it is a genuine idle stall, the
    # inbox block is already persisted as the floor. Either way, leaving it beats
    # sabotaging the queue. Delivered, unconfirmed.
    emit "pushed-unverified"
    ;;
  *)
    # Composer is clean (empty) and no busy marker. Either the agent already
    # finished a trivial turn, or the message landed without us catching the
    # transition. Nothing is staged, so this is safe -- delivered, unconfirmed.
    emit "pushed-unverified"
    ;;
esac
